WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.153
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Cambridge exams.

2
00:00:05.453 --> 00:00:09.176
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.476 --> 00:00:18.177
Unit two: A2 Key: Reading Texts and Gap-fill. Today you will read short texts, match people to texts and complete gaps.

4
00:00:18.477 --> 00:00:21.164
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:25.298
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:26.965
Match.

7
00:00:27.950 --> 00:00:30.576
Match means to connect two things that go together.

8
00:00:31.183 --> 00:00:33.171
Match each person to a text.

9
00:00:33.783 --> 00:00:36.122
Your turn. Say: Match.

10
00:00:38.967 --> 00:00:39.782
Match.

11
00:00:40.517 --> 00:00:41.251
Gap.

12
00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:44.609
Gap means an empty space in a text.

13
00:00:45.217 --> 00:00:46.218
Fill in the gap.

14
00:00:46.817 --> 00:00:49.666
Your turn. Say: Gap.

15
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:53.211
Gap.

16
00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:54.823
Option.

17
00:00:55.817 --> 00:00:58.072
Option means one possible answer.

18
00:00:58.683 --> 00:01:00.021
Choose option B.

19
00:01:00.617 --> 00:01:03.095
Your turn. Say: Option.

20
00:01:05.933 --> 00:01:06.807
Option.

21
00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:08.539
Article.

22
00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:12.467
Article means a short piece of writing in a magazine.

23
00:01:13.083 --> 00:01:15.222
I read an article about sports.

24
00:01:15.817 --> 00:01:18.167
Your turn. Say: Article.

25
00:01:21.033 --> 00:01:21.976
Article.

26
00:01:22.717 --> 00:01:23.567
Advert.

27
00:01:24.583 --> 00:01:27.012
Advert means a short message selling something.

28
00:01:27.617 --> 00:01:29.245
The advert is for a bike.

29
00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:32.073
Your turn. Say: Advert.

30
00:01:34.933 --> 00:01:35.842
Advert.

31
00:01:36.583 --> 00:01:37.445
Prefer.

32
00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:40.322
Prefer means to like one thing more.

33
00:01:40.917 --> 00:01:42.254
I prefer tea.

34
00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:45.502
Your turn. Say: Prefer.

35
00:01:48.367 --> 00:01:49.228
Prefer.

36
00:01:49.983 --> 00:01:50.706
Cheap.

37
00:01:51.717 --> 00:01:53.496
Cheap means not expensive.

38
00:01:54.083 --> 00:01:55.398
A cheap ticket.

39
00:01:55.983 --> 00:01:58.334
Your turn. Say: Cheap.

40
00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:02.004
Cheap.

41
00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:03.751
Delicious.

42
00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:06.993
Delicious means tasting very good.

43
00:02:07.583 --> 00:02:08.944
A delicious meal.

44
00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:12.562
Your turn. Say: Delicious.

45
00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:16.482
Delicious.

46
00:02:17.267 --> 00:02:20.906
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:24.226
Visal, what do the middle Reading parts ask?

48
00:02:25.317 --> 00:02:34.108
You match people to short texts, read a longer text and answer questions, and complete a short text with the correct word from three options.

49
00:02:35.217 --> 00:02:37.263
How do I match a person to a text?

50
00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:49.559
Underline what the person wants, for example a cheap holiday near the sea. Then check each text for the same ideas, and make sure every detail matches.

51
00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:52.336
How do I complete the gaps?

52
00:02:53.450 --> 00:03:03.324
Read the whole text first. Then look at the words before and after the gap, because grammar and meaning must both fit.

53
00:03:04.467 --> 00:03:05.944
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:06.783 --> 00:03:11.233
Grammar focus: Strategy: underline needs, check every detail.

55
00:03:11.533 --> 00:03:12.696
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:13.383 --> 00:03:16.091
Sam wants a cheap holiday near the sea.

57
00:03:16.983 --> 00:03:20.063
I need a word after 'a' that is a noun or adjective.

58
00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:28.166
Underline what each person wants, check every detail in the texts and read the finished sentence to test each gap.

59
00:03:28.917 --> 00:03:32.776
Be careful! Don't say: I check only one detail.

60
00:03:34.733 --> 00:03:36.744
Say: I check every detail.

61
00:03:37.044 --> 00:03:38.951
All details must match.

62
00:03:39.767 --> 00:03:42.361
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

63
00:03:43.167 --> 00:03:44.899
Matching People to Texts.

64
00:03:45.199 --> 00:03:47.559
Three friends look at holiday adverts.

65
00:03:48.567 --> 00:03:51.077
Sam wants a cheap holiday near the sea.

66
00:03:52.067 --> 00:03:55.192
Mia prefers a quiet town with delicious food.

67
00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:59.790
The first advert is expensive, so it is not for Sam.

68
00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:03.601
Sam chooses the one that matches all his needs.

69
00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:08.690
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

70
00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:11.782
In a matching task you check blank.

71
00:04:12.082 --> 00:04:16.304
A, only the title. B, nothing. C, every detail.

72
00:04:19.867 --> 00:04:22.632
The answer is C: every detail.

73
00:04:23.783 --> 00:04:25.017
What does Sam want?

74
00:04:25.317 --> 00:04:31.013
A, a cheap holiday near the sea. B, a mountain trip. C, a city job.

75
00:04:34.567 --> 00:04:37.692
The answer is A: a cheap holiday near the sea.

76
00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:40.176
Cheap means blank.

77
00:04:40.476 --> 00:04:44.536
A, old. B, not expensive. C, very big.

78
00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:50.877
The answer is B: not expensive.

79
00:04:52.017 --> 00:04:54.376
What do you do before choosing a gap word?

80
00:04:54.676 --> 00:04:59.421
A, guess quickly. B, skip. C, read the whole text.

81
00:05:02.967 --> 00:05:05.419
The answer is C: read the whole text.

82
00:05:06.583 --> 00:05:08.026
Mia prefers blank.

83
00:05:08.326 --> 00:05:13.709
A, a quiet town with good food. B, a noisy party. C, a long flight.

84
00:05:17.267 --> 00:05:20.102
The answer is A: a quiet town with good food.

85
00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:24.301
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

86
00:05:25.150 --> 00:05:27.022
How do you match people to texts?

87
00:05:35.817 --> 00:05:37.363
Now listen to Visal.

88
00:05:37.663 --> 00:05:48.887
First I underline what each person wants. Then I look at each text and check all the details. I choose the text that matches everything, not only one part.

89
00:05:49.187 --> 00:05:51.325
Excellent answer, Visal!

90
00:05:52.217 --> 00:05:58.213
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and strategy: underline needs, check every detail.

91
00:05:58.513 --> 00:06:03.777
Today's value is precision of language. Choose your words carefully so others understand you.

92
00:06:04.077 --> 00:06:06.602
See you in Unit three. Goodbye!

93
00:06:06.902 --> 00:06:10.297
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

